WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:08.080
This car goes 250 miles per hour.
This train goes 350 miles per hour.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.280
This plane goes 550 miles per hour.
And those are all slower than this bullet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.840
which travels at 800 miles per hour.
But this new plane from Boom Supersonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.120
goes over 1,000 miles per hour, and there’s a good&nbsp;
chance it could change how we travel forever.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.320
The only faster way to get around might be&nbsp;
using a SpaceX rocket, but Elon’s plan for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.240
that is still completely untested.
Supersonic planes have been around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.200
since the 1960s, but we’ll get into the&nbsp;
history of high-speed travel a bit later.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.320
First, we need to talk about&nbsp;
the founder of Boom Supersonic.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.240
Blake Scholl is on a mission to&nbsp;
bring supersonic travel to the masses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.840
and he’s making solid progress.
“Imagine New York to London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.600
in three and a half hours for about the&nbsp;
same fare you pay in business class today.”

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.960
In just 5 years he’s raised&nbsp;
hundreds of millions of dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.080
hired dozens of engineers,&nbsp;
and built a working prototype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.480
called XB-1 that can travel at Mach 2.2.
It’s amazing progress, but the most impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.800
part is that he had zero experience in the&nbsp;
aerospace industry. So how’d he do it?

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.680
Even though Blake doesn’t have an aerospace&nbsp;
background, he is an engineer by training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.800
He studied computer science in college and then&nbsp;
went to work for Amazon all the way back in 2001.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.440
While there, he built an automated system that&nbsp;
would buy Google ads for Amazon products. 

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.360
“That turned into a $300 million&nbsp;
dollar P&amp;L by the time I was 24”

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.680
Joining a high-growth startup like Amazon in the&nbsp;
early days is one of the best ways to accelerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.560
your career. No big company would hire a 24 year&nbsp;
old to manage a $300 million dollar piece of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.440
business, but by joining a risky venture, Blake&nbsp;
was able to develop management skills that helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.280
make Boom a reality later on.
After a few years at Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.840
Blake wanted to start his own company, so&nbsp;
he launched an app called Barcode Hero.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.880
“I’m Blake Scholl, I’m the&nbsp;
co-founder and CEO of Barcode Hero"
&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.320
“Barcode Hero lets you zap a&nbsp;
product barcode with your phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.680
to find a competitor’s pricing, share&nbsp;
your thoughts or recommendations”

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.640
The app received positive reviews and was&nbsp;
growing steadily, but mobile commerce was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.960
extremely competitive market back in 2010.&nbsp;
It’s now extremely common for startups to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.520
spend years completely focused on building&nbsp;
a great product before trying to make money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.760
but back then, questions about&nbsp;
profitability popped up much earlier.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.480
“Can you tell me how far along you need to be&nbsp;
before you make a profit? Well, to be honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.840
we’re a ways away from that. Our business model&nbsp;
is to create a shopping application that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.680
want to use when they’re in a store to help&nbsp;
them make better decisions about what to buy.”

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.440
So in 2012, Groupon acquired Blake’s company for&nbsp;
an undisclosed sum and shut down Barcode Hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.760
It doesn’t look like it was an amazing&nbsp;
outcome, but Blake clearly wasn’t deterred.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.720
He wanted to go bigger with his next startup&nbsp;
and so he set his sights higher, literally.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.080
Blake had been flying planes since he was&nbsp;
in college and always dreamt of having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.840
real impact on the aviation industry. He&nbsp;
didn’t have the perfect resume to start a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.640
company that builds airplanes, but that didn’t&nbsp;
bother him. After all, the Wright Brothers had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.280
only built bicycles before they made the first&nbsp;
airplane. And Elon Musk was running a payments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.680
company before launching SpaceX.
So Blake founded Boom Supersonic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.000
began building a team around the idea of&nbsp;
safe and sustainable high-speed travel.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.040
One of the first major milestones for the company&nbsp;
was joining the startup accelerator Y Combinator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.040
Although YC is typically known for&nbsp;
funding software startups like Airbnb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.080
Stripe, and Coinbase, the partners at Y&nbsp;
Combinator love all ambitious entrepreneurs. 

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.800
Building physical things is usually much harder&nbsp;
than writing software. There are long research and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.760
development cycles, expensive manufacturing&nbsp;
bills, and plenty of regulatory hurdles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.760
But Blake developed a really interesting&nbsp;
method for overcoming these challenges.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:46.320
“At Boom, we believe that life happens in&nbsp;
person, and that real face time beats FaceTime”

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.800
Blake is obsessed with the idea that in person&nbsp;
interactions are inherently more valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.520
than conversations over the phone or email. 
And he really lives this mantra. Y Combinator’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.080
Demo Day was quickly approaching and Blake was&nbsp;
going to have to present Boom to a huge group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.080
of investors. He needed to demonstrate that&nbsp;
airlines would actually buy the supersonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.000
planes he was going to build. Emails and&nbsp;
phone calls to airlines were going nowhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.720
so he decided to hop on a plane and get some face&nbsp;
time with Richard Branson. Virgin Galactic was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.560
rolling out a new Spaceship and Blake knew&nbsp;
that this was an incredible opportunity.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.840
“We ended up crashing the rollout! I never got&nbsp;
my name on the invite list, but we got our 15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.160
minutes with Richard Branson. We’re not asking&nbsp;
you to invest in the company, we’re if when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.600
airplane is delivered whether you would like the&nbsp;
first few with the Virgin logo on the tail.”

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.000
This was a huge moment for the company. Everyone&nbsp;
knows Richard Branson and Virgin airlines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.040
so overnight Boom became much more legitimate&nbsp;
in the eyes of investors. Most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.640
Virgin didn’t have to actually spend any money up&nbsp;
front, so it was easy to get the deal approved.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.280
I was actually at the YC Demo Day where&nbsp;
Blake first presented Boom to investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.920
and the reaction was pretty amazing. Everyone&nbsp;
loved the idea and couldn’t wait to invest.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.000
None of the specifications of the airplane had&nbsp;
been decided yet, after all, the company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.200
still brand new. But Blake had a clear vision&nbsp;
for what would be most important to airlines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.760
Speed, Safety, and Sustainability.
These might seem like vague marketing terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.320
but they are actually extremely calculated&nbsp;
based on the previous failures of supersonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.360
development. In order to understand why Blake&nbsp;
is so focused on these particular values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.000
we have to go back to 1969.
America had just landed a man on the moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.440
and the first commercial supersonic&nbsp;
airplane was about to take flight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.200
It was the Concorde and it had a lot&nbsp;
in common with Boom’s new aircraft.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.160
On the Concorde, a trip from New York to London&nbsp;
took just over three hours and it felt like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.960
dawn of an entirely new age. Throughout human&nbsp;
history, increases in the speed of transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.760
have had remarkable second order effects. In&nbsp;
the 1840s, traveling across the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.360
required traveling in a covered wagon. The journey&nbsp;
took months and people often died in transit.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:54.880
But in 1869, the transcontinental railroad&nbsp;
shortened that trip to just 83 hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.120
America went through a remarkable period&nbsp;
of growth as people migrated westward. 

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:05.360
And in the 1930s transcontinental airlines began&nbsp;
moving Americans across the country in just 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:12.160
hours. A few decades later, jet engines cut that&nbsp;
time down to just 6 hours and travel exploded.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:17.840
The jet age affected culture in remarkable ways.&nbsp;
The Beatles were able to go on the first world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:23.040
tour and become an international sensation and&nbsp;
Major League Baseball became a national sport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:29.040
played all over the United States. It’s always&nbsp;
hard to predict the effects of faster travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.280
but throughout history, great things&nbsp;
always happen when we start moving faster.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.040
The Concorde promised to make international&nbsp;
travel dramatically more accessible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.960
but there were a ton of problems, and most of them&nbsp;
come back to speed, safety, and sustainability.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.960
Speed was obviously the main selling&nbsp;
point of the Concorde. Business travelers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.200
value their time extremely highly and are&nbsp;
willing to pay lots of money to save time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.920
but it’s also just cool to go fast. And&nbsp;
celebrities loved how fast the Concorde went:

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.080
“It’s always exciting to go supersonic&nbsp;
and it’s always exciting to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.440
to New York before you’ve left.”
But traveling at Mach 2 can cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.640
some real issues. The first of which was heat. 
The Concorde went so fast that the plane would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.840
heat up substantially. The entire fuselage had&nbsp;
to be coated in a highly reflective white paint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.560
in order to deflect as much light as possible.&nbsp;
One Concorde was painted blue during a promotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:24.480
for Pepsi and wound up only being able to stay at&nbsp;
Mach 2 for 20 minutes because of heat build up! 
&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.040
The Concorde was built before computer simulation&nbsp;
allowed for engineers to test performance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.320
virtual environments, so a lot of these issues had&nbsp;
to be worked out through painstaking experiments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.760
But Boom is building their planes&nbsp;
using the latest software tools.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.560
The second issue was fuel. You would expect&nbsp;
a plane that goes twice as fast to use twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.960
as much fuel. But the Concorde actually&nbsp;
used more than five times as much fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.440
as modern commercial airliners. This meant&nbsp;
that tickets had to be extremely expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.320
to offset the cost of fuel and every trip&nbsp;
came with significant carbon emissions. 

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.320
Boom is leveraging advances in engine&nbsp;
design that yield greater fuel efficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.480
which will mean cheaper ticket prices. But&nbsp;
they aren’t stopping there. Boom plans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:09.120
use fuel made from carbon that was removed&nbsp;
from the atmosphere, meaning that supersonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.160
travel will be carbon neutral from day one.
Increased fuel efficiency won’t just make New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.120
York to London trips more affordable though.&nbsp;
It will actually open up entirely new routes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.640
The Concorde was never able to fly across the&nbsp;
pacific, but Boom has planned to offer service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:30.240
from San Francisco to Tokyo since day one. This&nbsp;
new route will expand the market for these planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.320
and give Boom a better chance at success.
Blake also thinks about pricing differently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.320
than the executives who ran the Concorde&nbsp;
project. He wants to continually bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.800
prices down until you can get anywhere in&nbsp;
the world for just a hundred bucks. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.760
Concorde executives were much more focused on&nbsp;
short-term profitability. British Airways and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:53.360
Air France actually performed market research to&nbsp;
inform their pricing strategy. People generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.880
thought of the Concorde as extremely expensive,&nbsp;
so they raised prices to match expectations!

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.360
This is fine when your customer is a rich business&nbsp;
traveler, but if you’re catering to wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.640
people, you need to have a perfect safety record,&nbsp;
and safety is what really did the Concorde in. 

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:14.400
"News tonight this Tuesday, the first Concorde&nbsp;
ever to crash just after takeoff from paris.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.880
113 people are killed, which&nbsp;
included everyone on-board."

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.920
Shortly after the turn of the millennium, Air&nbsp;
France Flight 4590 crashed during takeoff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.840
killed everyone on board. The plane had run over a&nbsp;
piece of debris on the runway and ruptured a tire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.920
It destroyed the Concorde’s reputation&nbsp;
and grounded the entire fleet for months.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.880
One year later, the September 11th terrorist&nbsp;
attacks crushed the entire airline industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:45.440
and forced the Concorde into&nbsp;
retirement shortly after.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.680
But Boom is learning from all of this.&nbsp;
Airplane safety protocols have advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.360
significantly in the past two decades&nbsp;
and the problem with the Concorde’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.840
tires has been thoroughly investigated. 
Many of the safety issues with the Concorde&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.920
came from the fact that the engineers on the&nbsp;
project had a goal of delivering a supersonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:07.200
plane at essentially any cost. The original&nbsp;
budget for the Concorde was 70 million, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.920
project wound up costing more than a billion. 
Engineers had to make lots of compromises to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:16.880
the Concorde to work. One of which was the nose&nbsp;
of the airplane. Because the plane landed at such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:22.160
a steep angle, the pilots couldn’t see the runway&nbsp;
as they approached. So the entire nose cone of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.800
plane would tilt down during landing. Boom just&nbsp;
installed a camera on the front of the plane. 

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.560
“There’s a camera toggle button you can hit&nbsp;
and it flips over and you get all your flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.600
controls overlaid on top of what is a virtual&nbsp;
window through the nose of the airplane. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.240
you get better runway visibility that you&nbsp;
get with a traditional subsonic cockpit.”

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.200
Such a simple solution that&nbsp;
significantly reduces complexity.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:48.640
Another issue that plagued the Concorde was the&nbsp;
fact that it required a flight engineer to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:53.520
onboard during every flight. Essentially, the&nbsp;
plane was so complex that a trained expert had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:58.240
to sit in the cockpit with the pilots just to&nbsp;
ensure everything went smoothly. This created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:03.360
a ton of extra cost, and even with the flight&nbsp;
engineer, most airlines still had to have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:09.840
extra Concorde sitting on standby at all times.&nbsp;
This was because customers were so demanding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.640
no airline could afford to frustrate customers&nbsp;
with delays if there were maintenance issues.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.280
Boom is building for reliability from day&nbsp;
one, and by simplifying the design of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:24.400
plane and using software solutions wherever&nbsp;
possible, they should be able to avoid many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:29.440
of the pitfalls that the Concorde faced.
Lastly, there’s the issue of noise. The fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.880
that Blake named his company Boom is pretty funny,&nbsp;
since the sonic boom was one of the major issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:39.440
with Concorde. Sonic booms are so powerful&nbsp;
that they can break windows in houses below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.000
and for that reason, the United States has&nbsp;
put a speed limit on aircraft traveling over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.160
the country. But NASA engineers are working on&nbsp;
solving the issue of the sonic boom as well. 

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.920
It’s unlikely that they will ever be able&nbsp;
to muffle it entirely, but there’s a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:57.920
chance that they could lower the boom from 105&nbsp;
decibels to just 70 decibels. At that volume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.080
US lawmakers might revisit the speed&nbsp;
limit if it means getting from California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.920
to Washington DC in half the time.
Strict regulation is just one of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.160
factors that have led to stagnation in the&nbsp;
aerospace industry and prominent investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:14.320
like Peter Thiel have been calling for&nbsp;
America to address this for years now:

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.120
“I often think it's worth looking at quantity&nbsp;
not quality and we're not going faster you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:25.280
the Concorde was decommissioned in 2003 and if&nbsp;
you include low tech airport security mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.440
we're back to about 1960 type&nbsp;
travel speeds today in the US”

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:33.920
Part of the problem with the Concorde was&nbsp;
that, ultimately, it was a vanity project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.160
by the UK and French governments. They had&nbsp;
amazing engineers working on the technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:43.360
but they never had a strong economic model. But&nbsp;
Boom is thinking of the business model from day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:49.440
one, and they already have signed contracts with&nbsp;
airlines. Only 14 Concordes ever saw commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:54.640
service, but United Airlines has already&nbsp;
committed to buying 20 planes from Boom. 

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:58.320
That’s really solid progress, but&nbsp;
Boom has some strong competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:03.680
Subsonic planes are getting faster and more&nbsp;
efficient. It’s also possible to have a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:08.480
productive flight on a traditional plane now&nbsp;
that you can use the WiFi to get some work done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:12.800
or sleep in a lie flat seat in first class.
There are also other companies working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:17.760
on developing supersonic planes, like&nbsp;
Spike Aerospace and Aerion Supersonic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:22.800
although Aerion recently went out of business.
But I think Boom’s real competition will come from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:27.840
outside the traditional airline industry. There&nbsp;
are several companies working on planes capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:33.120
vertical takeoff and landing which could change&nbsp;
the way we travel, even if everything is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.760
subsonic. A huge amount of any trip is&nbsp;
spent just getting to and from the airport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:42.240
and these new VTOL aircraft will make it&nbsp;
much easier to get to smaller airports.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:46.880
Then there’s SpaceX’s point-to-point. Elon&nbsp;
Musk thinks he can get passengers from New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:51.040
York to Tokyo in just 30 minutes using&nbsp;
a rocket. This is clearly a long-shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.520
but if it works, it would be substantially&nbsp;
faster than flying in a supersonic plane.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:59.200
Lastly, we can’t forget about the exponential&nbsp;
growth of the internet. Every year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:03.840
internet speeds get faster and more people get&nbsp;
connected. Video conferencing just saw a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:08.640
surge in adoption during the pandemic and people&nbsp;
are more comfortable doing business remotely now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:12.960
Virtual reality is still in its infancy, but&nbsp;
Facebook is working hard to make communication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:17.200
in VR indistinguishable from reality. This&nbsp;
is all part of a larger initiative to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.480
what people are calling a “metaverse”,&nbsp;
but that’s a topic for another video.

